<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Mamdot State</h1><p class="st-meta">1800 CE – 1855 CE (approx.) · capital Mamdot</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Pathan Nawabi state on the Sutlej, granted by Ranjit Singh to the Kasur family and taken over by the British in 1855.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasur">Kasur</a> around 1807, he compensated its Pathan ruling family with the lands of Mamdot on the south bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-ud-din-khan-of-kasur">Qutb-ud-din Khan</a> and his descendants ruled there as Nawabs.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Mamdot later passed under British protection. Nawab Jamal-ud-din Khan supported the British in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-sikh-war">First Anglo-Sikh War</a> in 1845, but was deprived of ruling powers in 1855 for misgovernment, after which the family held Mamdot as a large estate.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After 1855 Mamdot was part of British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firozpur-district">Ferozepur district</a>. The family stayed prominent: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iftikhar-hussain-khan-mamdot">Iftikhar Hussain Khan Mamdot</a> became the first Chief Minister of West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Pakistan</a> in 1947.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="princely state, 1800 CE – 1855 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">princely state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Indian princely states</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:95.52%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl 